insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 1, 'A decline suite generates a clean synthetic history from known parameters, fits it, and confirms the fit returns those parameters to fourteen decimal places. What class of defect can that round-trip test never detect?', '["Any error in the rate-cumulative relation, because the fitter regresses rates alone", "A window filter that silently discards the first and the last rows of the supplied rate history", "A sign error in the linearised regression that recovers Di from the slope of the fitted log-rate line", "A model-selection rule that ranks the three candidates on R2 rather than on RMSE"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fitter never evaluates the cumulative formula, so a defect there leaves every round-trip assertion green. Regression sign errors, window handling and selection rules all do change the recovered parameters, which is exactly what a round-trip test compares. This gap is what the literature anchors exist to close.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 2, 'Before the SC1 oracle work of 2026-07-18, the hyperbolic branch of calculateEUR carried a defect that shipped in production code. What was it?', '["It divided by (b - 1) rather than (1 - b), so it returned a negative EUR for every b other than exactly 1", "It applied the harmonic form (qi/Di)ln(qi/qL) for every b above 1, overstating shale gas volumes", "It raised qi to the power (1 - b) instead of b in the leading factor, so EUR scaled with the economic limit", "It returned zero whenever qi exceeded the stated economic limit by more than two orders of magnitude"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The denominator sign was inverted, so the whole expression changed sign for any b not equal to 1. The comment now in engines/dca/arps.js records the fix and names the three oracles that pin it: the closed form, a Simpson quadrature and a hand-arithmetic case.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 3, 'The dca literature fixture states a rounding doctrine in its _notes block. What does it say about how published rates and published cumulatives differ?', '["Both columns are rounded identically, so a single absolute tolerance covers rates and cumulatives alike", "Printed rates are rounded, while printed cumulatives come from the author''s own unrounded spreadsheet", "Printed cumulatives are rounded to the nearest hundred units while rates are given to full precision", "Rates are typed as published but cumulatives are recomputed by the fixture author before being committed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because of that asymmetry the tests recompute the unrounded end rate from the rate equation and feed that into the cumulative, then compare rates with an absolute allowance and cumulatives with a relative one. Recomputing a published cumulative would destroy its value as an oracle.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 4, 'Case ced-p03-004-exponential-oil gives qi = 150 bbl/d and an effective annual decline of 0.15, and publishes q = 92.1 bbl/d at three years with a rate_abs tolerance of 0.05. What does the engine return, and does the case pass?', '["91.94 bbl/d, a gap of 0.16, which fails until the nominal decline is recomputed from the secant convention", "92.1 bbl/d exactly, because 150 times 0.85 cubed terminates and needs no tolerance", "92.11875 bbl/d from 150 times 0.85 cubed, a gap of 0.0187500000000114, which passes", "92.65 bbl/d, a gap of 0.55, which passes only because the case widens rate_abs for exponential examples"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The nominal decline is -ln(1 - 0.15) = 0.16251892949777494 per year, and 150 times 0.85 cubed gives 92.11875 bbl/d. That is a terminating decimal but it is not equal to the printed 92.1, which is why the tolerance exists at all.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 5, 'In the Weaver harmonic table the printed rate at half a year is 99 bbl/d and the printed cumulative is 19833 bbl. An analyst pushes the printed 99 through the harmonic cumulative instead of the unrounded 98.7336213411464. What happens?', '["The result is 19833.4237325343 bbl, matching the engine, because the harmonic cumulative is insensitive to rate", "The result is unchanged to six significant figures, so the doctrine only matters for the b = 1.2 gas case", "The result is 20106.9 bbl, high by about 274, which passes because np_rel of 0.0002 dominates np_abs of 25", "The result is 19559.477832566186 bbl, low by 273.5221674338136, which fails the case''s np_abs tolerance of 25"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rounding the rate to whole barrels moves the cumulative by hundreds of barrels, more than ten times the case''s absolute allowance. The same substitution at one year misses by 156.95335835550213 low and at five years by 128.04256681443076 high, and that change of sign down the column is the signature of rounding rather than of a bug.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 6, 'Case ced-p03-004-hyperbolic-b115-cumulative states qi = 4563 bbl/month, di = 0.0545 per month, b = 1.15 and t = 109 months. How should the engine be run against it?', '["Feed the monthly values directly, because nominal declines scale linearly and the Arps forms are unit-agnostic", "Convert only di to a per-day value, since rates are always daily but declines carry the document''s own unit", "Convert qi to a daily rate and di to a per-day value, then evaluate at 3315 days to match the printed rate", "Refit the monthly table with the engine''s grid first, because 1.15 is not a rung the search can ever return"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Arps forms hold as long as qi, Di and t agree on a unit. Run in months the engine returns 762.062115355870 bbl/month against a printed 762, and a cumulative of 171883.377500690 bbl against a printed 171883. Dividing that rate by 365/12 gives 25.0540969432067 bbl/d, and mistaking the column for a daily rate would be wrong by a factor of thirty with no fit statistic disturbed.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 7, 'The Weaver shale gas case prints two cumulatives for the same volume, 1014165 Mscf from the rate form and 1014083 Mscf from the time form. Which does the fixture assert against, and why?', '["The rate form, because it is the larger value and a conservative oracle should be hard to pass", "The time form, whose inputs are unrounded, unlike the rate form''s 876 Mscf/d end rate", "Neither, because the document''s own 82 Mscf disagreement disqualifies the case from being armed", "The mean of the two, 1014124 Mscf, with the 82 Mscf spread carried as the case''s absolute tolerance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rate form inherits the rounding of the printed 876 Mscf/d end rate while the time form does not. Pushing the rounded 876 through the cumulative returns 1014143.50535155 Mscf, which is 68.07342120993417 above the engine''s unrounded 1014075.43193034 and lands close to the rate form, confirming the document''s own explanation of the spread.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 8, 'For the Weaver shale gas parameters the EUR is computed at successively lower economic limits: 100, 10, 1 and 0.1 Mscf/d. What distinguishes the b = 1.2 column from the b = 1 column?', '["At b = 1 the volumes converge on a finite ceiling near 8802015.75736088 Mscf, while at b = 1.2 they grow linearly", "At b = 1 each decade adds the same 2001395.1395436698 Mscf, while at b = 1.2 each decade adds more than the last", "Both columns converge, but the b = 1.2 column converges roughly three and a half times higher than b = 1", "At b = 1.2 each decade adds the same 3927256.81730133 Mscf, while at b = 1 the increments shrink toward zero"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic cumulative is logarithmic in the limit, so equal ratios of limit add equal volumes forever. Above b = 1 the increments grow instead, which is why no ultimate recovery exists for that case: the well reaches 100 Mscf/d after 36.9835258918630 years and 10 Mscf/d only after 597.936247555473 years.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 9, 'Case ahmed-reh-16-1-exponential-rate-cum carries two declines in its given block, a graph-point value of 0.000418 per day and a least-squares value of 0.000401 per day. What does the case note record about their use?', '["Both are averaged to 0.0004095 per day before any assertion is made, which is why gp_rel is as loose as 0.005", "The graph value is a superseded first pass and only the least-squares value of 0.000401 is used anywhere", "The part-b time answers use the least-squares value, while the part-a cumulative uses the graph-point read", "The graph value applies to the 184 MMscf/d time answer and the least-squares value to the 3637 day answer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With the least-squares decline the times come out at 1560.36383981150 and 3637.44394688159 days against printed integers of 1560 and 3637. With the graph decline the cumulative at the 80 MMscf/d limit is 631578.947368421 MMscf, inside the gp_rel of 0.005, while the least-squares value gives 658354.114713217, off by 0.0390689941812137.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 10, 'Month 5 of the Ahmed Example 16-2 forecast table prints a cumulative of 4643 MMscf where the model gives 5642.497777231364. How does the fixture handle it?', '["The printed value is corrected to 5642 in the table and a comment records the original for traceability", "The row is deleted from published_table so that no assertion can be written against a corrupted value", "The case''s gp_abs tolerance is widened from 6 to 1000 so that every row of the printed table passes as typed", "The value stays verbatim, a misprint field states the evidence, and the row is excluded from Gp assertions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A cumulative that goes backwards is impossible, and the gap of almost exactly 999 reads as a dropped digit. The rate assertion on that same row still runs. Correcting the number, deleting the row, or loosening the tolerance would each quietly destroy the value of the oracle.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 11, 'Ahmed Example 16-2 runs entirely in months with qi = 1240 MMscf/month and Di = 0.0383 per month, and publishes a life of 97 months and a cumulative of 31600 MMscf at a 30 MMscf/month limit. What does the engine return?', '["97.1715215910425 months and 31592.6892950392 MMscf, each inside the tolerance the case states for it", "2954 days and 31592.6892950392 MMscf, the first requiring conversion at 365/12 days per month before comparison", "97.1715215910425 months and 32348.5639686684 MMscf, the cumulative failing until the misprinted month 5 row is dropped", "88.6 months and 31600 MMscf exactly, because the book''s least-squares decline of 3.48325/91 must be used unrounded"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The time is within the case''s t_econ_abs of 0.5 and the cumulative is within its gp_econ_rel of 0.001 at a relative error of 0.000231351422811298. Nothing is converted to days at any point, because nominal declines scale linearly with the time unit.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 12, 'The Ikoku case note warns about the decline value used to build the published forecast table. What is the warning?', '["The book prints Di in per-year units while the table was built in per-month units, a factor of twelve on every row", "The text shows a daily Di rounded to 0.001, but the table was computed with the unrounded 0.00100493150684932", "The book''s Di of 0.3668 is a secant effective decline and must be converted before any row can be reproduced", "The Di was solved by the same Newton iteration as b, so it inherits the iteration''s convergence tolerance of 1e-6"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rounded 0.001 is about half a percent off every row while the unrounded value reproduces the table to roughly 3e-5. Holding the book''s parameters, the worst relative error across all 25 published rate rows is 4.97309481548787e-5 against a rate_rel tolerance of 0.0002.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 13, 'The book fits Ikoku''s gas well by Newton iteration and prints b = 0.5195. Our engine scans b by repeated addition of 0.05. What are the two rungs that bracket the published value?', '["0.5195 is itself a rung, because the engine refines its step to 0.0005 once a bracket has been established", "0.5 and 0.55 exactly, since the accumulated float departs from the decimal only above b = 1", "0.49999999999999994 and 0.5499999999999999, the accumulated floats rather than the tidy decimals", "0.44999999999999996 and 0.5499999999999999, since the loop skips any rung within 0.001 of the harmonic case"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Repeated addition of 0.05 produces accumulated floats rather than the decimals you would write, and there is no refinement pass. The harmonic skip applies only within 0.001 of b = 1, nowhere near this bracket, so both neighbouring rungs are evaluated and the lower one wins.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 14, 'The 25 published Ikoku rate rows are handed to fitArpsModel as dated data. The engine returns qi = 9.713071524956366 MMscf/d and Di = 0.34843194718482895 per year against the book''s 10 and 0.3668. What does that shift show?', '["The published table must contain a transcription error, since a correct table recovers the stated qi", "The engine''s regression is biased low because it fits log rates rather than rates on the original scale", "Arps parameters trade against each other, so denied the exponent it wanted the fit moves qi and Di", "The fit was run on the cumulative column by mistake, which always drives qi below the true initial rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Forced onto a rung below the Newton value, the fit lowered qi by nearly three percent and Di by five percent to buy back some of the misfit. The result still reproduces the published 20-year cumulative to within 0.33892212335496076 percent, which is why a single fitted parameter cannot be read as an estimate of a physical quantity.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-literature-anchors', 15, 'The fixture file lists Poston and Poe, Analysis of Production Decline Curves (SPE, 2008) under pending_references rather than among its armed cases. Why does that entry exist?', '["Its worked examples duplicate the Ahmed chapter 16 cases, so arming it would add no independent coverage", "Its examples use a decline formulation the engine does not implement, so no assertion could be written", "It was armed and then retired after two of its rows disagreed with the SPEE REP #6 closed forms", "Every located channel for the document is paid, so nothing has been typed and the jest todo stays visible"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Recording an unmet gap as an explicit entry keeps it in the test report every run. The contrast is SPEE REP #6, which sat on the same pending list until it was found to carry an explicit reproduction grant, and was then sourced, typed and armed.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 1, 'At qi = 120 stb/d, Di = 0.0012 per day and a 10 stb/d economic limit, what EUR does the engine return for b = 1.2?', '["321875.914758613 stb, computed from the Arps rate-cumulative form running on its b > 1 branch", "248490.664978800 stb, since above b = 1 the engine falls back to the harmonic closed form", "91666.6666666667 stb, because an economic limit of 10 stb/d makes the exponent immaterial", "185086.013635292 stb, the value the grid returns once b has been quantised to the nearest 0.25 rung"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is the graded capstone field b12_eur_stb, carried at a tolerance of 2000 stb. The other three values are real entries from the same b table at b = 1, b = 0 and b = 0.75, which is exactly why the exponent has to be read carefully before a booking is copied.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 2, 'On the same well the b = 1.2 booking is compared with the exponential booking fitted from identical early data. What is the ratio?', '["3.5113736155485027, so the exponent alone more than triples the booked reserve", "1.5519815245204083, the ratio the b = 0.5 row of the same table carries", "2.7108072543141826, because the harmonic case sets the upper bound on the ratio", "12.000000000000002, the ratio at the b = 2 limit of the engine''s default search range"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing 321875.914758613 by 91666.6666666667 gives the graded capstone field b_ratio at a tolerance of 0.02. Nothing about the well changed between the two bookings except the curvature of the decline.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 3, 'The EUR-versus-b table is stepped in quarters of b, and each quarter multiplies the previous booking by 1.228944276581309, then 1.2628575225865635, then 1.3009949647258734, then 1.3425685717584586. What does that pattern mean for uncertainty in b?', '["It cancels out, because the multipliers are all near 1.3 and a fixed band on b maps to a fixed band on EUR", "It grows with b, so the same band of uncertainty is worth steadily more volume at the top of the range", "It is an artefact of the 10 stb/d limit and disappears entirely once the limit is lowered to 5 stb/d", "It shrinks with b, so an uncertainty of one rung is worth least where the exponent is largest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The response is convex. In grid units, one 0.05 rung from b = 0.45 to b = 0.50 is worth 6800.834890331229 stb, while one rung from b = 1.15 to b = 1.20 is worth 20741.890432329266 stb, three times as much volume for the same movement in the parameter.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 4, 'Raising b from 0 to 1.2 on this well moves the EUR from 91666.6666666667 to 321875.914758613 stb. Through what mechanism does the extra volume arrive?', '["Through higher rates across the observed history, which run 30 percent above the exponential inside the first year", "Through a higher fitted qi, since the hyperbolic branch scales the initial rate by (1 + b) before integrating", "Through a much longer life, since time to the limit stretches from 2070.75554149000 to 13003.4874682 days", "Through the economic limit, which the engine relaxes from 10 to 1 stb/d for any b above unity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The b = 1.2 well is still above the limit after more than 35 years where the exponential well died at about 5.67. The additional barrels are late, low-rate barrels bought by extending life, not by producing faster in any period the data covers.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 5, 'Evaluate the b = 0 and b = 1.2 curves for this well at 90 days. By how much do the two rates differ, and what does that imply?', '["By 15.970448 stb/d, about 32 percent, so a quarter of history separates the two models cleanly", "By 0.087534425 stb/d, about 0.076 percent, which is why the two models are separable only at day 30", "By 6.9553804 stb/d, about 8.98 percent, so three months of data can reject one of them", "By 0.69668507 stb/d, about 0.647 percent of rate, comfortably inside ordinary production scatter"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At three months the two curves that differ by a factor of 3.51 in booking differ by two thirds of one percent in rate. The gap only reaches 2.4733288 percent at six months and 31.957869 percent at two years, so short windows carry almost no information about the exponent.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 6, 'The exponential booking on this well reaches its 10 stb/d limit at 2070.75554149000 days. What fraction of the b = 1.2 booking is produced after that day?', '["About 59.77 percent, so the bulk of the incremental reserve lies beyond any history you could observe", "About 13.05 percent, matching the booking gap a fixed-b type curve match produces on Ekene-6", "About 40.23 percent, which is the share the hyperbolic curve delivers before the exponential well dies", "About 89.05 percent, since a hyperbolic at b above 1 delivers almost nothing in its first six years"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At that day the b = 1.2 curve has produced 129484.214773766 stb of its 321875.914758613 stb booking, leaving 192391.699984847 stb, or 59.7720087659024 percent, in a period beyond the economic life of the alternative model.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 7, 'On the day the exponential well hits its 10 stb/d limit, what is the b = 1.2 curve still producing?', '["10.0090706091916 stb/d, since both curves must meet the same limit on the same day by construction", "37.9408492439197 stb/d, nearly four times the economic limit and comfortably economic", "54.5019190573 stb/d, the rate the same curve carries at three years", "20.091081478993967 stb/d, the rate at which a 5 percent terminal decline would take over"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hyperbolic curve is still comfortably economic at 2070.75554149000 days, which is why so much of its booking lies beyond that point. The other rates quoted are real values from the same well at other times or under a terminal-decline cap.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 8, 'Check the b = 1 row of the table by hand from the harmonic closed form at qi = 120 stb/d, Di = 0.0012 per day and a 10 stb/d limit. What do you get?', '["100000 times ln(11), giving 239789.527279837 stb, since the limit enters the logarithm as a difference", "100000 times ln(12) = 2.4849066497880004, giving 248490.66497880008 stb", "(120 - 10)/0.0012 times ln(12), giving 227790.988929421 stb", "120 times ln(12)/0.0012, giving 248490.66497880008 stb only if the rate is first converted to stb/yr"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic EUR is (qi/Di)ln(qi/qL), so 120/0.0012 = 100000 multiplied by ln(12) = 2.4849066497880004. The ratio of that to the exponential booking of 91666.66666666667 stb is 2.7108072543141826.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 9, 'Why is it wrong to treat b as one parameter of three, each carrying roughly a third of the uncertainty in an Arps booking?', '["Because a five percent error in Di moves the exponential EUR by twenty five percent, far more than b can", "Because the engine fits qi and Di jointly and b separately, so they carry unequal freedom", "Because qi and Di are read from visible features of the history while b is inferred from curvature", "Because b is bounded to the interval 0 to 2 by the search range while qi and Di are unbounded above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A five percent error in qi or in Di moves the booking by roughly five percent and is arguable from the chart. One 0.05 rung of b near the top of the range moves it by 20741.890432329266 stb on a well whose exponential booking is 91666.6666666667 stb, and nothing in the data displays the quantity being estimated.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 10, 'What does SPEE REP #6 Table 1 actually tabulate, and how was it verified before being committed to the fixture?', '["Recommended maximum b values by play, cross-checked against the reserves disclosures of eleven operators", "Terminal decline percentages by flow regime, verified against the four Weaver cases already in the file", "Economic limit rates by fluid type and region, validated against the Ahmed chapter 16 worked examples", "Tangent and secant effective declines against nominal decline, checked row by row against the REP''s closed forms"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Thirty seven rows give tangent effective decline and secant effective decline at b of 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5 and 2. The rows were extracted mechanically from the PDF text layer and then verified against De = 1 - e^-D and Desi = 1 - (1 + bDi)^(-1/b), because an extraction can drop a digit as easily as a person can.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 11, 'Compare the spread of Table 1''s decline conventions at a nominal decline of 10 percent per year with the spread at 100 percent per year. What is the governance point?', '["The spread widens from 0.8033511964040496 points to 20.947082882855803 points, so convention outweighs fit", "The spread narrows at high decline, from 20.947082882855803 points down to 0.8033511964040496 points", "The spread is constant at about 0.8 percentage points, so the convention never affects a booking materially", "The spread is irrelevant above b = 1, since the secant convention is undefined for exponents that steep"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 10 percent nominal the tangent value is 9.51625819640405 and the b = 2 secant value is 8.712907. At 100 percent nominal those become 63.21205588285580 and 42.264973. At shale-scale declines an unstated convention moves the answer more than the fit does, which is why a recommended practice exists to define the words.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 12, 'A well is reported as declining at 65 percent a year with no convention stated, and it is hyperbolic at b = 1.2. What nominal decline does that phrase imply?', '["1.04982212449868 per year, since -ln(1 - de) is the only conversion the Arps family admits for any b", "Either 1.04982212449868 or 2.10389088694457 per year, depending on which convention was meant", "0.65 per year, because effective and nominal declines coincide when the exponent exceeds unity", "2.10389088694457 per year, since the secant convention is mandatory for any exponent above 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read as a tangent effective decline the nominal is -ln(1 - 0.65). Read as a secant effective decline at b = 1.2 it is ((1 - 0.65)^-1.2 - 1)/1.2, twice as large. The reporting rule is a triple: the effective decline, its convention, and the b it was computed at.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 13, 'A terminal decline of 5 percent per year is applied to the b = 1.2 forecast on this well. At what point does the forecast switch to an exponential tail?', '["At 2070.75554149000 days, the day the matching exponential booking reaches its economic limit", "At 13003.4874682 days, the day the uncapped hyperbolic curve itself reaches the 10 stb/d limit", "At 5235.505470031922 days, where the instantaneous decline has fallen to 0.00014052957366452213 per day", "At 4964.726625845288 days, the duration the exponential tail runs before the limit is reached"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The switch time is (Di/Dmin - 1)/(b*Di) with Dmin = -ln(1 - 0.05)/365. That lands at 14.343850602827185 years, with the rate at the joint equal to 20.091081478993967 stb/d. The other times quoted are real values from the same case in different roles.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 14, 'Capping that b = 1.2 forecast with a 5 percent per year terminal decline changes the booking from 321875.914758613 stb to what, and what should be concluded?', '["To 226180.603901891 stb, a cut of about 30 percent that brings the booking within 10 percent of exponential", "To 91666.6666666667 stb, because a terminal decline forces the hyperbolic case back onto the exponential result", "To 286642.170692589 stb, a cut of about 11 percent which nonetheless still books 3.13 times the exponential", "To 243306.489069931 stb, a cut of about 2 percent, since a 5 percent floor bites hardest at high exponents"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The hyperbolic segment contributes 214834.64138858786 stb to the switch and the exponential tail adds 71807.52930400119 stb, for a total of 286642.170692589 stb over 27.9458413585677 years. Finite is not the same as small: the capped booking is still 3.1270054984646074 times the exponential.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-b-lever', 15, 'Your reserves policy requires a terminal decline on every hyperbolic forecast. What does our decline engine provide for that?', '["A minDecline constraint on fitArpsModel, which caps the tail whenever the fitted b exceeds 1", "A facilityLimit setting in generateForecast, which serves as the terminal-decline floor for hyperbolic cases", "A two-segment mode in calculateEUR, entered by passing the model type as hyperbolic-to-exponential", "Nothing, so the two segments must be computed outside the engine and the result labelled accordingly"]'::jsonb, 3, 'calculateEUR takes qi, Di, b, the economic limit and a model name, and generateForecast steps a single Arps segment to the limit. There is no Dmin argument and no switch anywhere in engines/dca/arps.js, so every EUR the panels report is an unbounded hyperbolic booking and must be described as one.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 1, 'A fit returns a confidence interval block. What quantity is that block actually a statement about?', '["The scatter of the data about the fitted curve, under the family you chose", "The spread of EUR outcomes implied by the economic limit and the decline exponent together", "The distance between the fitted parameters and the true parameters, at 95 percent coverage", "Which of the three Arps families the window supports"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The half-widths are built from the residual variance of the linearised regression, so they describe how consistently the points sit on the assumed curve. Distance from truth is unknowable to the estimator, EUR spread involves the limit and b in ways no regression sees, and family selection is done on RMSE outside the interval machinery entirely.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 2, 'Ekene-1''s primary-window fit reports a qi half-width of 2.74326145200774e-14 stb/d. Why is it that small?', '["The residuals are zero, so the residual variance and both standard errors are zero", "The 36 monthly rows span 1096 days, which is long enough to pin qi to fourteen decimals", "The RMSE of 1.42601125915484e-14 is copied straight into the qi field", "Auto-Select chose Exponential, whose branch reports a qi half-width of zero by definition"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ekene rows lie exactly on the planted decline, so the sum of squared residuals in log space is 4.33873497871556e-30 and the residual variance that drives both standard errors is 1.27609852315164e-31. What remains is floating-point noise. The exponential branch computes a real half-width whenever scatter exists, window length alone never buys fourteen decimals, and RMSE is not used in the interval formula.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 3, 'linearRegressionWithSE divides the sum of squared residuals by n minus 2 before taking standard errors. What does that denominator represent?', '["A conservative correction that matches the two reasonableness bounds, 2 times qi and 5 times Di", "The residual variance with the two fitted parameters removed from the degrees of freedom", "The exclusion of the first and last row of the window from the residual sum", "The two sided nature of a 95 percent interval"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A straight line consumes two degrees of freedom, the slope and the intercept, so the unbiased residual variance divides by n minus 2. It has nothing to do with the reasonableness bounds, no rows are discarded, and two sidedness is handled by the 1.96 multiplier.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 4, 'How does the engine compute the standard error of the regression slope?', '["The residual variance multiplied by one over n plus the squared mean time divided by Sxx", "The RMSE on the rate scale divided by the square root of n minus two", "The square root of the residual variance divided by Sxx, the time-axis spread", "The residual variance divided by the number of points in the window"]'::jsonb, 2, 'SE of the slope is the square root of the residual variance over Sxx, where Sxx is the sum of squared deviations of time from its mean. The form built from one over n plus the squared mean time is the intercept variance rather than the slope variance, RMSE lives on the rate scale and is never used here, and dividing by n instead of Sxx ignores how far apart the observation times are.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 5, 'computeConfidenceIntervals fixes its multiplier at 1.96. What is the consequence on a fit with 11 points?', '["The interval is exact, because 1.96 is the two sided 95 percent Student t value at 9 degrees of freedom", "The interval is 3.68594435294176 percent too narrow, which is the shortfall that applies at 34 degrees of freedom", "Nothing at all, because the multiplier cancels out of the delta method transforms", "It is a normal approximation, so the band is 15.4161817754041 percent too narrow"]'::jsonb, 3, '1.96 is the normal quantile. The residual variance is estimated from the data, so the correct multiplier is the Student t value at n minus 2 degrees of freedom, which is 2.26215716279792 for 9. The 3.68594435294176 percent figure belongs to Ekene-1''s 36 row window, and the multiplier scales every half-width directly rather than cancelling.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 6, 'Fitting Ekene-1''s primary window on only its first four monthly rows returns no interval block at all. Which rule fired?', '["The sample size gate, which returns hasIntervals false whenever n is below 5", "The reasonableness check, because the Di half-width exceeded 5 times Di on so few points", "The b placeholder rule, which suppresses all three half-widths when b is fixed at zero", "The window rule, which requires at least one row after the flood start"]'::jsonb, 0, 'computeConfidenceIntervals returns hasIntervals false when either standard error is not finite or when n is under 5. Adding a fifth row makes the block appear, with a qi half-width of 1.62165554600328e-13. The reasonableness bounds are a separate later test, the b rule sets a placeholder rather than suppressing anything, and no flood-related rule exists.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 7, 'What are the two reasonableness bounds that decide whether a computed interval is reported at all?', '["R2 must be at least 0.95 and RMSE must be below one percent of qi over the window", "The qi half-width must be under 2 times qi and the Di half-width under 5 times Di", "The qi half-width must be under 10 percent of qi and the b half-width under 10 percent of b", "Both half-widths must be smaller than the parameters themselves"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The check is qiHalfWidth below 2 times qi and DiHalfWidth below 5 times Di, and failing either one returns hasIntervals false. The quality bands play no part, the ten percent figure is the hard-coded b placeholder rather than a bound, and the bounds are deliberately much looser than the parameters themselves.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 8, 'The hyperbolic branch reports a b half-width. Where does that number come from?', '["Propagating the intercept standard error through the q to the power minus b transform by the delta method", "A hard-coded 10 percent of b, because the grid search gives no regression error for b", "The width of the grid step, 0.05, applied symmetrically about the selected b", "The spread of RMSE across the grid values that fit within one percent of the best"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The source sets bHalfWidth to b times 0.10 with a comment explaining that b comes from the grid search and cannot be given an interval from the regression. The value 0.0500000000000000 on Ekene-3 is ten percent of 0.5 and coincidentally equals the grid step, which is exactly the confusion to avoid. No RMSE scan or transform propagation is performed for b.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 9, 'In the harmonic branch, the two contributions to the Di half-width, 0.0000117433016325249 and 0.00000922000436706834 on the Weaver table, are combined how?', '["Added in quadrature, giving 0.0000149302918176847, the standard variance propagation result", "The larger of the two is taken and the smaller discarded as a second order effect", "Added in absolute value, giving the reported 0.0000209633059995933", "Averaged, then scaled by the 1.96 multiplier a second time"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The code sums the qi contribution and the slope contribution in absolute value before multiplying by 1.96, which is 1.40407878530295 times wider than the quadrature sum a textbook variance propagation would give. Nothing is discarded or averaged, and applying 1.96 twice is a feature of the hyperbolic branch rather than the harmonic one.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 10, 'On the Ikoku hyperbolic fit, 78.5979258620400 percent of the reported Di half-width sits in a term that carries the 1.96 factor twice. Why?', '["Because the branch uses a 95 percent level on qi and a 99 percent level on Di", "Because the grid search evaluates each candidate b twice, once for RMSE and once for R2", "Because the delta method requires squaring the multiplier for a two parameter transform", "The qi term reuses qiHalfWidth, which already carries the 1.96 multiplier"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hyperbolic Di expression reuses the already-scaled qiHalfWidth inside its qi term and then multiplies the sum by the multiplier once more, so that term carries 1.96 squared. On the Ikoku table this makes the reported half-width 1.62593544665970 times what a single application would give. The delta method does not square the multiplier, and only one confidence level is used anywhere in the function.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 11, 'Weaver''s harmonic table gives a Di half-width of 0.0000209633059995933, but refitting the same eleven times with unrounded rates from the same curve collapses it to 9.42256562313961e-19. What does that tell you?', '["The interval measured the author''s printing convention, not anything about the reservoir", "The engine loses precision on rounded inputs, so the first fit understates the true interval", "The harmonic branch is numerically unstable for qi values near 120 bbl/d", "Eleven points is below the sample size gate, so the first result should be discarded"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The only difference between the two fits is that the printed column was rounded to whole barrels per day. That round-off is the entire residual scatter, so it is the entire interval. Precision is not lost, the branch is perfectly stable, and eleven points is comfortably above the gate of five.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 12, 'Forcing an exponential onto Ekene-3''s primary window gives Di 0.00136416266227741 per day with a half-width of 0.0000435124771665498. The planted truth is 0.002. What is the lesson?', '["The interval is too wide to be useful, so the fit should be rejected on that basis", "The truth sits 14.6127588941636 half-widths outside a band the estimator reports as tight", "The truth is inside the band once the Student t correction of 3.92517058468751 percent is applied", "The half-width should have been suppressed by the 5 times Di reasonableness bound"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A tight interval around a badly displaced parameter is exactly what a scatter-based estimator produces when the family is wrong: the points agree with each other under the assumed exponential, and the assumption is false. No t correction of any size closes a gap of nearly fifteen half-widths, and the bound is nowhere near being triggered by a half-width of about three percent of Di.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 13, 'Ekene-1''s invalid full-history fit reports a Di of 0.00196150586036441 per day with a half-width of 0.00261952695412852, yet hasIntervals is true. What is wrong with the reported band, and why was it published?', '["Its upper end exceeds the planted decline of 0.0012 per day, which ought to have triggered an internal truth check", "Nothing is wrong: a half-width larger than the parameter is normal for a 72 row window", "Its lower end is negative, describing a growing well, and both loose bounds still passed", "It was published because b reached the 1.95 ceiling, which disables the reasonableness test"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The band runs from -0.000658021093764111 to 0.00458103281449293 per day, and a negative nominal decline is not a decline at all. It was published because the ratio of half-width to Di is 1.33546730961174 against a bound of 5. No estimator can compare itself against a planted truth, and hitting the b ceiling does not disable any check.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 14, 'Fitting Ekene-1 as an exponential over the twelve months from 2023-09-01 returns hasIntervals false. Which test failed, and by how much?', '["The qi test, because the qi half-width reached 7.71329862832121 times the fitted qi", "The sample size gate, because a twelve month window carries fewer than five usable monthly rows", "The Di test, at 7.71329862832121 times Di against a bound of 5", "Both tests failed together, since R2 over that window fell to 0.00760679640972350"]'::jsonb, 2, 'On that window the qi ratio is only 0.0373890935079556 and passes easily, while the Di half-width of 0.000189235269532259 is 7.71329862832121 times Di and fails the bound of 5. The window has twelve rows, and R2 is not an input to either test even though it is very low here.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-confidence-and-scatter', 15, 'The engine''s fit of the Ikoku table returns Di 0.348477790253015 per year with a half-width of 1.32345157098809 percent, while the published Di is 0.3668 per year. Why does the interval miss the book?', '["The published table is rounded to six decimals, which inflates the residual scatter of the fit", "The book reports an effective decline while the engine reports a nominal one", "Twenty five rows is too few for a normal approximation at 23 degrees of freedom", "The grid landed on b 0.5 instead of the book''s 0.5195, a choice residuals cannot see"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine scans b in steps of 0.05 and so cannot reach the Newton-solved 0.5195, and with b held at 0.5 the other two parameters bend to compensate. That is a modelling choice, invisible to the vertical residuals the interval is built from. Six decimals leaves negligible round-off, both figures are nominal, and the t correction at 23 degrees of freedom is far too small to bridge the gap.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 1, 'Why is a parameter confidence interval an inadequate substitute for a reserves range?', '["Because it propagates only residual scatter, not family, window or limit choices", "Because a 95 percent level is too narrow for reserves work, which reports at 80 percent", "Because it is computed on the linearised scale rather than on the rate scale directly", "Because reserves ranges must be symmetric about the deterministic booking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The half-widths come from vertical residuals under a fixed model, so they cannot carry the decisions that actually move a booking: which family was fitted, which window was chosen, what economic limit was assumed, and what decline exponent governance permits. The confidence level, the regression scale and symmetry are all beside the point.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 2, 'In the petroleum convention used throughout this course, what does P90 denote?', '["The low case: a 90 percent chance of recovering at least that much", "The value with a 90 percent chance of being exceeded on the upside within the bracket", "The 90 percent confidence bound on the fitted qi, transferred to a volume", "The 90th percentile of the distribution, so the high case that only 10 percent of outcomes beat"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The P number counts probability of exceedance, so a larger P is a smaller volume. P90 is the 10th percentile of the distribution and is found by solving F(x) equal to 0.10. The high case is P10, and no P label refers to a regression confidence bound.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 3, 'Why is the Ekene field range built at field level rather than by summing four well-level distributions?', '["Because the four wells share the closed-form EUR sum 461709.132532792 stb as their individual mode", "Because quantiles do not add and the dominant uncertainties are common mode across the four wells", "Because the group roll-up already reports 461475.535264973 stb, which is a distribution", "Because Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 are injectors and cannot be booked"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sum of four P90s is not the P90 of the sum except under perfect correlation, and these wells share one window doctrine, one b policy, one limit and one flood model, so they miss together rather than independently. The closed-form sum is the field mode rather than any well''s mode, the roll-up is a single number, and the injectors are irrelevant to the aggregation question.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 4, 'For a triangular distribution with minimum a, mode m and maximum b, what is F evaluated at the mode, and what is its value on the Ekene triangle?', '["It is always 0.5, since the mode splits a triangular distribution into equal areas", "It is 2 divided by b minus a, which gives 0.0000100000000000000 per stb here", "It is m minus a over b minus a, which gives 0.408545662663958 here", "It is one minus the ratio of the two arms, which gives 0.591454337336040 here"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Substituting x equal to m into the lower branch cancels the m minus a factor and leaves the mode''s position as a fraction of the full range, 81709.1325327920 over 200000. The peak height of the density is 2 over b minus a, a different quantity with different units, and 0.591454337336040 is the probability of exceeding the mode rather than F at it.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 5, 'You need a quantile from a triangular distribution. What decides which of the two inverse formulas to use?', '["Whether the target volume is above or below the arithmetic mean of a, m and b", "Whether p is below 0.5, since the median always coincides with the mode", "Whether the distribution is right skewed, since left skewed cases always use the upper branch", "Whether the target probability p is at or below F evaluated at the mode"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The branch rule compares p with the threshold F at the mode. Below or equal to it, use the lower form built on a; above it, use the upper form built on b. The mean plays no part, skew direction only shifts where the threshold sits, and the median coincides with the mode only for a symmetric triangle.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 6, 'On the Ekene triangle, min 380000, mode 461709.132532792, max 580000 stb, how is the P90 obtained?', '["Add the square root of 0.10 times 200000 times 81709.1325327920 to the minimum", "Subtract one standard deviation of 41051.8343295015 stb from the mean of 473903.044177597 stb", "Interpolate 10 percent of the way from the minimum to the mode along the density", "Subtract the square root of 0.10 times 200000 times 118290.867467208 from the maximum"]'::jsonb, 0, 'P90 means p equal to 0.10, which is below the threshold 0.408545662663958, so the lower branch applies: 380000 plus the square root of 1634182650.65584, which is 380000 plus 40425.0250544862. Subtracting a root built on the b minus m arm is the P10 construction instead, and neither a standard deviation offset nor a linear interpolation solves a quadratic CDF.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 7, 'Which construction gives the Ekene triangle''s P10, the high case?', '["580000 minus the square root of 0.50 times 200000 times 118290.867467208", "580000 minus the square root of 0.10 times 200000 times 118290.867467208", "380000 plus the square root of 0.90 times 200000 times 81709.1325327920", "461709.132532792 plus the square root of 0.90 times 200000 times 118290.867467208"]'::jsonb, 1, 'P10 means p equal to 0.90, above the threshold, so the upper branch applies with 1 minus p equal to 0.10: 580000 minus the square root of 2365817349.34416, which is 580000 minus 48639.6684748587 and gives 531360.331525141 stb. Using 0.50 in the same form produces the P50 instead, and the lower branch is not available at this probability.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 8, 'On the Ekene triangle the mode is 461709.132532792 stb and the P50 is 471238.394887162 stb. What does that ordering tell you?', '["That the mode was computed at a 20 stb/d limit while the P50 assumes 10 stb/d", "That the triangle is right skewed, so the median sits 9529.26235437050 stb above the peak", "That the triangle is misspecified, because the median must equal the mode", "That the P50 was taken from the lower branch by mistake"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The right arm of 118290.867467208 stb is longer than the left arm of 81709.1325327920 stb, so probability accumulates more slowly above the peak and the median lands above it. Median and mode coincide only for a symmetric triangle, all quantiles here use the same 10 stb/d basis, and p equal to 0.50 correctly uses the upper branch given a threshold of 0.408545662663958.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 9, 'Which number should an expected-value economics run use for the Ekene field, and how does it relate to the median?', '["The deterministic booking 461709.132532792 stb, which is the engine''s own answer", "The P50 of 471238.394887162 stb, since expected value and median agree on a triangle", "The mean of 473903.044177597 stb, which exceeds the P50 by 2664.64929043484 stb", "The midpoint of the bracket, 480000 stb, which averages the minimum and maximum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mean of a triangular distribution is the simple average of its three inputs, and on a right-skewed triangle it exceeds the median. The deterministic booking is the mode, which sits lower still at 12193.9116448053 stb below the mean, expected value and median only agree under symmetry, and the bracket midpoint ignores the mode entirely.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 10, 'Where does the Ekene triangle''s mode of 461709.132532792 stb come from?', '["The engine''s group roll-up of the four base scenarios at a 10 stb/d limit", "The mean of the four wells'' EURs at limits of 5, 10 and 20 stb/d", "An elicited most likely case agreed with the asset team, like the minimum and maximum", "The sum of the four producers'' closed-form EURs at the 10 stb/d limit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is 91666.6666666667 plus 111270.166537926 plus 153505.672866270 plus 105266.626461929, each well booked with the analytic rate-cumulative relation for its own family on its primary window. The engine roll-up gives a slightly different figure, no averaging across limits is involved, and unlike the two bounds the mode is calculated rather than elicited.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 11, 'The engine''s group roll-up of the four base scenarios reports 461475.535264973 stb against a closed-form sum of 461709.132532792 stb. What is that difference, and why does the triangle use the closed form?', '["It is 233.597267818579 stb of forecast discretization, and a mode should not move when a time step changes", "It is 233.597267818579 stb of flood response that the roll-up includes and the closed form omits", "It is a rounding artifact of the 15 significant figure print, so either value is equally valid", "It is the effect of the 5 stb/d limit, which the roll-up applies to Ekene-5 only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The roll-up sums daily forecast rates, a right-endpoint rectangular sum that understates a declining integral by 0.0505940323374476 percent here. The closed form is exact and independent of any forecast setting, which is why it anchors the mode. Neither the flood response nor a different economic limit is involved, and the gap is far too large to be a printing artifact.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 12, 'Raising the Ekene triangle''s maximum from 580000 to 620000 stb moves the P10 by 27003.7956473161 stb and the P90 by only 3858.37116562098 stb. What general point does that make?', '["That the maximum is the only input that matters once the mode is calculated rather than elicited", "That each headline quantile is dominated by the bound on its own side of the distribution", "That the triangular distribution is unstable and a lognormal should be used instead", "That the P90 is insensitive to every input, which is why it is the safest number to report"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Moving the minimum from 380000 to 400000 stb does the mirror image, lifting the P90 by 12903.1069902332 stb and the P10 by only 2496.02737004613 stb. The mode moves all three least, by 2402.33361559565, 4698.70708130661 and 2101.32568803220 stb for a 10000 stb shift, so the range is largely a record of two elicited bounds rather than an unstable distribution or an insensitive P90.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 13, 'Why does this course grade closed-form triangular quantiles rather than Monte Carlo output?', '["Because the repository''s simulator has no forecast model, only a parameter sampler", "Because Monte Carlo is only valid for lognormal inputs and the field triangle is bounded", "Because engines/dca/monteCarlo.js draws from unseeded Math.random, so no run reproduces another", "Because sampling is too slow to run inside a graded capstone submission"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both the Box-Muller normal draw and the uniform draw call Math.random, which takes no seed in JavaScript, so a graded field would take a different value on every submission. The module does generate forecasts, sampling works for any input distribution, and run time is not the objection.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 14, 'At the engine''s default of 1000 iterations, what is the standard error of a sampled P90 on the Ekene triangle?', '["41051.8343295015 stb, the standard deviation of the distribution itself", "606.375375817294 stb, which is the value that applies once the iteration count reaches 10000", "1917.52730462323 stb, which is 0.958763652311616 of the 2000 stb capstone tolerance", "It cannot be quantified, since the sampler is unseeded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The sampling error of an empirical quantile is the square root of p times 1 minus p over n, divided by the density at the quantile, which is 0.00000494743045255836 per stb at the P90 here. Reaching 606.375375817294 stb needs ten times as many iterations, the distribution''s own standard deviation is a different quantity, and being unseeded affects reproducibility rather than computability.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-risked-reserves', 15, 'What does sampleArpsParameters in engines/dca/monteCarlo.js assume about the fitted parameters when it draws them?', '["That qi, Di and b share a covariance matrix recovered from the regression design", "That each parameter is uniform across its reported interval, with b clamped between 0 and 2", "That the confidence interval half-width is one standard deviation of a normal draw", "That each half-width is two standard deviations and the three parameters are independent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It divides each half-width by 2 to get a standard deviation and then draws qi, Di and b separately, which contradicts the strong correlation between an intercept and a slope from the same regression line. No covariance is carried anywhere in the engine, the draws are normal rather than uniform, and treating the half-width as one standard deviation would widen the spread further still.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 1, 'Between the waterflood start and the end of its lag, what does an Ekene producer''s rate do?', '["It continues the primary Arps decline at a reduced nominal rate until injected water reaches the wellbore", "It rises linearly toward the lift multiplier over the lag months and then holds until breakthrough", "It holds exactly at the rate the primary decline had on 2023-01-01", "It falls at 0.00035 per day"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The response model clamps the ramp fraction to zero while months since the flood are below the lag, so the gross rate equals the frozen base. Ekene-5 repeats 41.390728476821195 stb/d on ten consecutive monthly rows, from 2023-01-01 through 2023-10-01.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 2, 'Ekene-6''s frozen base rate is 42.698178934617005 stb/d and its lift is 1.35. What is its gross rate on the ramp-end row, 2023-10-01?', '["42.698178934617005 stb/d, unchanged, because the lift only applies from breakthrough", "57.64254156173296 stb/d, the frozen base multiplied by the lift", "It cannot be found without the water cut on that date", "53.50156275634024 stb/d, which is the gross rate seven months later on 2024-05-01"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The peak of the response is the frozen base times the lift, and the fixture row for 2023-10-01 reads 57.64254156173296 stb/d. Water cut does not enter until breakthrough on 2024-03-01, and the post-ramp decline has not yet started.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 3, 'When does a flooded Ekene producer actually reach the water cut recorded in its `wcMax` field?', '["Only on the final history row of 2025-12-01, because the curve is normalised to that date", "At breakthrough itself, since wcMax is the initial water cut that the response model imposes on the stream", "Six months after breakthrough, after which it holds flat", "When the gross rate reaches the 10 stb/d economic limit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Water cut is a quadratic in time normalised between the breakthrough date and 2025-12-01, so wcMax is an endpoint value rather than a level the well sits at. Ekene-6 is at 0.004081632653061224 two months after breakthrough and 0.45 only on the last row.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 4, 'Ekene-6 lies 715.8910531638176 m from Ekene-2 and Ekene-5 lies 1767.0597047072292 m from its nearest injector. What does the fixture do with that geometry?', '["It gives Ekene-5 the larger lift, since a longer flow path sweeps more of the pattern before arriving", "Nothing: the lags are assigned per well and are independent of position", "It makes Ekene-6 respond first but break through last, because a short well-swept path delays water arrival", "It makes Ekene-6 respond first, with a lag of 3 months against Ekene-5''s 9, and break through first as well"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Response order follows distance to the nearer injector with no exceptions: 3, 5, 6 and 9 months of lag for separations of about 716, 1209, 1217 and 1767 m. The nearest well also carries the largest lift, 1.35, and the earliest breakthrough, 2024-03-01.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 5, 'The `post_ramp_decline_per_day` field reads 0.00035 for all four producers. What does that shared value mean?', '["The four wells share it because they all carry a decline exponent b of 0.35", "It is the rate at which water cut climbs after breakthrough, so Ekene-5 never uses it", "It is a property of the flood rather than of any single well", "It is a fitted average of the four post-ramp windows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ramp length and the post-ramp decline belong to the flood, while lag, lift and the water-cut pair belong to the well. Four clean windows of different lengths and start dates recover 0.00035 per day independently, which makes it a field parameter measured four times.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 6, 'Ekene-6''s clean post-ramp window runs 2023-10-01 to 2024-02-01, only five rows. Why does it stop there?', '["Because the engine''s fit needs a minimum of five points and more rows would over-constrain the regression", "Because water breaks through on 2024-03-01 and the oil stream stops being the gross stream", "Because the recorded history ends there", "Because the well is shut in for a workover between 2024-02-01 and its water breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A window has two ends and the right-hand one is often set by a stream change rather than a drive change. History runs to 2025-12-01, so data availability is not the constraint; breakthrough is.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 7, 'Leaving one row of unfinished ramp in each window gives R2 0.09572812278817722 on Ekene-6 across 6 rows and 0.9886766989253964 on Ekene-5 across 22. What does that contrast establish?', '["That R2 becomes less able to expose a contaminated row as the window gets longer", "That Ekene-6''s ramp is steeper, so its contaminated row is a larger outlier than Ekene-5''s", "That six rows are too few to fit", "That the engine weights early rows more heavily on short windows, which amplifies the residual at the window start"]'::jsonb, 0, 'R2 averages residuals over the whole window, so one bad row in six dominates and one bad row in twenty-two is diluted by twenty-one good ones. The detector is weakest on the long histories that analysts trust most.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 8, 'Moving Ekene-5''s window start from 2024-04-01 to 2024-05-01 leaves Di at 0.00035 per day and moves qi from 47.59933774834437 to 47.10215945583964 stb/d. Why does only one of them move?', '["The engine renormalises qi to the mean rate of the window while leaving Di in absolute daily units", "Because 2024-04-01 is the ramp-end row and carries a residual ramp contribution that inflates qi without affecting the decline", "Dropping a row always lowers qi", "qi is the fitted rate at the window''s first row, while Di belongs to the regime"]'::jsonb, 3, 'That split is the whole stability diagnostic. A start you can shift by a month without moving the decline constant sits inside one regime, and the initial rate moves simply because the curve has run one more month downhill.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 9, 'On the 2024-05-01 window Ekene-5 returns Di 0.00035 per day and Ekene-6 returns 0.0013275893489185155 per day. The two share a tank, a flood and a window. What accounts for the factor of 3.793112425481473?', '["Ekene-6 sits closer to the injectors, so the flood front is depleting it faster", "Ekene-6''s lift of 1.35 against Ekene-5''s 1.15, which puts its curve on a steeper part of the exponential", "Water cut, which climbs toward 45 percent on Ekene-6 and stays at zero on Ekene-5", "A different fitted b"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing the analysis can see distinguishes the two wells on that window except the stream. Ekene-6 has water on all twenty rows and Ekene-5 has none, and dividing the water back out returns Ekene-6 to the field constant exactly.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 10, 'Dividing Ekene-6''s oil rates by (1 - wc) on the capstone window and refitting returns qi 53.50156275634024 stb/d, Di 0.0003500000000000002 per day and R2 1.00000000000000. What has that demonstrated?', '["That the water-cut curve is exponential rather than quadratic in normalised time", "That the gross stream obeys the flood''s decline and the whole oil-fit distortion is stream mixing", "That the oil fit was the better of the two estimates", "That the engine cannot fit an oil stream falling faster than 0.001 per day without a forced model family"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The reservoir was behaving identically to Ekene-5 the whole time. Ekene-3''s gross stream on the same window returns 0.00035000000000000005 per day at R2 1 as well, so the reconstruction is not a one-well coincidence.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 11, 'Ekene-6''s oil fit on the capstone window reports qi 58.134557068111256 stb/d, while the well''s oil rate on that window''s first row is 53.28318903080415 stb/d. Why does the fitted curve start above the data?', '["A straight semilog line covering a stream that steepens has to start high and finish low", "The engine anchors qi to the peak rate of the whole history, which is the 2023-10-01 ramp-end row", "Rounding in the b grid", "Because the exponential extrapolates backwards to the flood start before applying the window''s own t0 convention"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Water cut is quadratic in time, so it takes a bigger bite each month and the oil stream is not a straight line on a semilog axis. The same fit finishes low too, predicting 26.952769057741932 stb/d at day 579 against an actual 24.02809722062498.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 12, 'Ekene-1''s oil fit on the capstone window reports R2 0.9612773701829425 and a quality tier of Excellent, with a decline 24.57945758048064 percent above the reservoir''s. What should you conclude about the quality tile?', '["It is reliable here, because only six of the twenty rows carry water and that is below half the window", "It should have read Good", "It is computed from RMSE rather than R2, so it is not comparable across wells with different rate magnitudes", "It measures agreement with the rows supplied, not whether those rows describe the reservoir"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tier is a band on a fit statistic and has no way to know what stream the rows came from. Ekene-6, whose decline is wrong by a factor of nearly four, is certified Good on the same window.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 13, 'What does decline analysis actually hand across to material balance?', '["The fitted qi, Di and b of each producer, from which material balance regenerates the rates it needs", "The EUR at the economic limit", "The cumulative production history, and the convention used to build it", "The type curve and its normalisation constants, so the pressure history can be matched well by well"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Material balance never uses q(t) directly; it uses Np at the survey dates, which have nothing to do with your fit window. The convention matters because the same rate history yields different cumulatives by different routes.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 14, 'At the 2023-01-01 survey the Ekene goldens carry F of 317926.8424845837 rb and Et of 0.026190080907192072 rb/stb. What does dividing one by the other give?', '["12139208.107496822 rb, which must be divided by the Bo of 1.21589748101760 before it can be compared", "12139208.107496822 stb, matching the volumetric STOIIP to floating-point noise", "261475.039999678 stb, the cumulative production at that survey", "The recovery factor of 2.153971146093131 percent, which is what the volumetric answer is compared against"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Havlena-Odeh writes the material-balance equation as F = N Et, so the ratio at any survey is oil in place. Every survey returns the same value, which is why the six-point regression reports a slope of 12139208.107496837 stb at R2 1.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-declines-after-the-flood', 15, 'Pressure at the flood start is 2096.00826266700 psia against a bubble point of 2000 psia. What does that margin say about the timing of the waterflood?', '["Pressure maintenance was started with only 96.0082626669955 psi of margin left above the bubble point", "The reservoir was already below bubble point, so the flood was meant to redissolve liberated solution gas", "The margin is irrelevant, since floods start on a fixed calendar schedule", "It shows the flood could safely have waited, since 1103.9917373330045 psi of the original 3200 psia remained"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field had produced 261475.039999678 stb, a recovery factor of 2.153971146093131 percent, and spent 1103.9917373330045 psi doing it. At 422.2168729123678 psi per hundred thousand barrels the remaining margin was worth about twenty-three thousand more barrels.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 1, 'What does validating the engine against a published worked example actually produce for a submission?', '["A calibration factor that is applied to every subsequent fit from the same tool version", "A citable sentence naming the reference, the page and the agreement achieved", "Confidence that the fixture data is free of noise", "An adjustment to the quality bands, so that Excellent is only awarded above the R2 the reference achieved"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A field has no answer key, so the profession validates against printed inputs with printed answers. What survives the exercise is a statement that can be repeated after a version change and shown to an auditor who asks what the tool was checked against.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 2, 'The Expert regime log adds a column that the Professional version did not have. What is recorded in it?', '["The R2 the fit achieved on each candidate window, so boundaries can be ranked before one is chosen", "The economic limit in force at each date, since limits change with price", "The reviewer who approved each boundary and the date of approval", "What stream each regime is measured on, and what drives that stream"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-6''s log now carries first oil, the waterflood, the ramp completion and the water breakthrough, and the last of those changes what the meter is measuring rather than what the reservoir is doing. Missing it returns a decline 3.79 times the reservoir''s at a quality tier of Good.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 3, 'In the Expert workflow, how is a window boundary proved?', '["By shifting the start and checking that the decline constant does not move", "By showing that R2 on the chosen window is higher than on any neighbouring window", "By confirming that the fitted quality tier reaches Excellent on the chosen rows", "By confirming that the fitted qi lies within one percent of the first recorded rate in the window"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A statistic cannot detect the contamination it is averaging over. Four wells carrying one bad row each return R2 of 0.0957, 0.7299, 0.9638 and 0.9887, and the two that clear the Excellent band are the two whose declines are wrong by 7.06 and 3.66 percent.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 4, 'The Expert workflow separates two decisions that the Professional workflow ran together. Which pair?', '["Which family to fit and which grid step to search b on", "Which forecast convention to report and which economic limit to apply", "Which stream to fit and which stream to book", "Which window to use for the fit and which window to use for the cumulative handed to material balance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Arps describes the stream the reservoir controls, while revenue is attached to a different one once water arrives. When they differ, the conversion between them is a forecast in its own right and belongs in the model as a visible line.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 5, 'The Ekene-5 tolerance on the Expert capstone''s post-ramp decline is 0.000005 per day. What does that tolerance rule out?', '["Any fit that reports the value to fewer than five significant figures", "A window start of 2024-03-01, whose fitted decline misses by a factor of 2.5621018371390645", "Any window shorter than twenty monthly rows", "Reporting the value from a window starting 2024-04-01 instead of the capstone''s 2024-05-01 start"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tolerance is 1.43 percent of the graded value, which is tight enough to reject a window still carrying ramp even though its fit reads R2 0.988676698925396 and a quality tier of Excellent. Any clean start passes, because they all return the same constant.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 6, 'Why is Ekene-6''s oil decline on the capstone window a field that candidates fail while doing good engineering?', '["Because the tolerance of 0.00002 is narrower than the engine''s own convergence on a twenty-row window", "Because auto-select returns Hyperbolic there, so the exponential value has to be produced by forcing the family", "Because the window has to be shortened to 2024-09-01 before the fit stabilises", "Because the instinct after module 5 is to report the reservoir''s 0.00035 per day rather than the well''s oil fit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The field asks what a fit of that well''s oil stream on that window returns, which is 3.793112425481473 times the flood''s constant. Reporting the physically meaningful number instead of the one asked for is the characteristic failure, and the memo is where the distinction is explained.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 7, 'In the uncertainty explorer''s triangular block, what does the F(mode) tile warn you about?', '["That the mode is not the P50, because the cumulative probability at the mode is well below 0.5", "That the mode must be re-entered whenever the minimum or maximum is changed", "That the triangle is only valid when the mode lies exactly halfway between the minimum and the maximum", "That the P50 has to be computed by sampling, because no closed form exists for an asymmetric triangle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For the field triangle the value an engineer would naturally call the estimate sits low in its own distribution, and the mean sits above the mode because the distribution is skewed right. Reading the mode as the mid case is the error the tile exists to expose.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 8, 'Two of the capstone''s six fields are a volume and a ratio taken at a fixed qi of 120 stb/d, Di of 0.0012 per day and a 10 stb/d limit. What is being varied to produce them?', '["The economic limit, from 5 stb/d up to 20 stb/d", "The window length, from the primary window out to the full history", "The decline exponent b alone, moved on the panel''s slider to 1.20", "The forecast convention, from the engine''s daily sum to the closed form"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Everything except b is held fixed, so the whole change in booked volume comes from the tail that b extrapolates and none of it from any observed data. That is precisely why the profession has written governance around the parameter.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 9, 'What is the stated test of a governance memo?', '["That it fits on one page and names an approver", "That a stranger holding it, the data and the tool lands on your number rather than near it", "That every number in it is quoted to full double precision", "That it can be reconstructed from the panel tiles alone, without reference to any regime log or event history"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The memo has to support re-derivation rather than summary, because the person defending the booking may be a stranger reading it years later. Any clause that fails that test is a gap, and gaps get filled with somebody else''s assumptions.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 10, 'Which of these window clauses is defensible in a reserves memo?', '["Window chosen to capture the post-flood decline and reviewed by the asset team", "Window chosen because it returns the highest R2 of the candidates examined", "Window chosen to give twenty monthly rows, the minimum the engine needs for a stable exponential fit on a flooded producer", "Start moved to 2024-04-01, Di unchanged at 0.00035; start moved to 2024-03-01, Di 0.00033718949081430467"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clause has to record the experiment and its result, not the conclusion. A statement of intent tells a reviewer what you wanted the window to do and nothing about whether it does it.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 11, 'The memo clause on b asks for a sensitivity as well as a justification. What sensitivity?', '["How much the booking moves if b is one grid step higher", "How much R2 falls if b is held at zero instead of fitted", "How much the fitted qi moves across the b values the grid search examined", "How much the time to the economic limit changes between the daily-sum forecast and the closed form at the fitted b"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The grid steps b by 0.05, so the neighbouring value is the smallest change a reviewer can reasonably ask about. If the evidence constraining b is weak, the size of that step in booked barrels belongs in the submission.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 12, 'The Ekene-6 memo books 36257.11302016905 stb of oil, the smallest volume in the field. What does the memo say the number actually is?', '["A conservative case, with the gross-stream booking of 124290.17930382918 stb held as the upside", "A gross liquid forecast that has yet to be converted to oil", "A forecast of the oil stream under a continuation of the observed water-cut trend, not a statement about the reservoir", "The reservoir''s own booking, since the gross reconstruction is an estimate and the oil rates are measured"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same window''s gross stream returns the field''s flood decline of 0.00035 per day at R2 1, so the reservoir under this well is behaving normally. The volume embeds a water-cut trajectory nobody has forecast independently, and clause 6 has to say so.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 13, 'Why does the governance memo''s structure belong in front of you while the analysis is running, rather than afterwards?', '["Because the capstone grader checks the memo before it checks the six values", "Because a clause you cannot fill is found while there is still time to fill it", "Because reserves auditors require the memo to be dated before the fit", "Because writing the exclusion list first is the only way to keep the forecast convention consistent between wells"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A memo written last describes what you did; a memo written alongside changes what you do. Every gap in the worked Ekene-6 example was found by trying to write a clause rather than by reviewing the analysis after the fact.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 14, 'One number computed in this course reappears verbatim as an input to the next course in the series. Which is it?', '["The four producers'' cumulative at the flood start, 261475.03999967827 stb", "The sum of the four closed-form EURs at the 10 stb/d limit, 461709.132532792 stb", "The pooled type curve''s fitted b of 0.05", "Ekene-5''s post-ramp EUR of 106006.16987382741 stb, which sets the flood''s recoverable target"]'::jsonb, 0, 'That figure is four Arps integrals and one addition, and it is the last row of the material-balance history in the same fixture directory. Nothing else produced here crosses the boundary intact, and its quality is a decline-analysis responsibility.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-booking', 15, 'A real material balance disagrees with the mapped volumetric answer. Which three suspects does the course name?', '["The pressure gauges, the PVT correlation and the survey dates", "The economic limit, the decline exponent and the forecast convention", "The mapped volume, the drive model, and the cumulative production", "The net-to-gross, the connate water saturation and the oil formation volume factor used in the volumetric calculation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The disagreement is the most informative result in a study, and decline analysis owns exactly one of the three suspects. Knowing how a cumulative goes wrong lets you clear or convict that one quickly.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Why does the profession validate a decline tool against published worked examples rather than against field data?', '["Because published examples carry more scatter than fixture data, which stresses the fitting routine harder", "Because regulators require the specific examples used in the standard references", "Because a field has no answer key, while a printed example has printed inputs and a printed result", "Because field data is usually confidential"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Validation needs a case whose right answer is known independently of the tool. The Ekene fixture is the teaching substitute for that, and the literature fixtures are the professional one.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'SPEE REP #6 Table 1 gives, at a nominal decline of 10 percent per year, a tangent effective decline of 9.51625819640405 percent and a secant effective decline of 9.090909 percent. What distinguishes the two?', '["The tangent form exponentiates the nominal decline; the secant form is the one-year drop from qi", "The tangent form applies to hyperbolic declines while the secant form is defined only for exponential ones, which is why they diverge", "The tangent form is quoted per calendar year and the secant form per 360-day financial year", "The secant form already includes the economic limit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both describe the same 10 percent nominal decline, and neither is wrong. Reporting one where a reader expects the other moves a booked rate by about 0.4253491964040492 percentage points at this decline, which is why the definition has to be stated.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'An Expert validation record has to survive a change of tool version. What must it contain to do that?', '["A screenshot of the panel tiles at the time of the check", "The R2 achieved on the fixture and the quality tier awarded", "A note that the fit reproduced the reference to within the graded capstone tolerances of that field", "The reference, its edition, the page, the digits of agreement and the tool version checked"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The record has to let somebody else repeat the check. The armed fixtures cite to that level of precision, down to page ranges such as pp. 1253-1256 for the Ikoku example, precisely so that the comparison can be repeated.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Ahmed''s Example 16-3 fits Ikoku''s 1984 gas-well data and reports a decline exponent b of 0.5195. The engine''s grid search steps b by 0.05. What follows?', '["The engine cannot fit that dataset at all, because the search range excludes non-round exponents", "The engine can land at 0.50 or 0.55 but not on the book''s value, because the book solves for b rather than scanning it", "The engine will return 0.5195 after refinement, since the grid is only used to seed a Newton iteration", "The engine will return b at the search ceiling of 2, since a value between grid points forces the optimizer to the boundary"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A grid search reports the best point on its grid, and 0.5195 sits 0.0195 above the nearest one. Agreement with a published fit therefore has to be judged on the forecast, not on the exponent, whenever the two methods differ in kind.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Weaver''s harmonic worked example is presented as a table of daily rates. What has to be checked before reproducing it?', '["That the economic limit used in the book is the same as the one in your own booking", "That the printed rates have not been rounded, since a harmonic fit is unusually sensitive to rounding", "The time basis and rate units, since the engine works in nominal decline per day", "That the harmonic branch is available in the model selector rather than being reached only through b of 1"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most reproduction failures against literature are unit and time-basis failures rather than arithmetic ones. A decline quoted per year against an engine expecting per day differs by a factor of 365 before any curve is fitted.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Holding qi at 120 stb/d, Di at 0.0012 per day and the limit at 10 stb/d, the booked EUR is 91666.6666666667 stb at b of 0 and 248490.664978800 stb at b of 1. What has changed between those two numbers?', '["Only the decline exponent, so the extra volume is entirely in the extrapolated tail", "The decline exponent and the effective annual decline, which the harmonic form recomputes", "The decline exponent and the time to the economic limit, which is what actually carries the volume", "The decline exponent and the fitted initial rate, since a harmonic curve cannot pass through the same first point"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Everything except b is fixed by construction in that comparison. The 2.710807254314181 times increase comes from a part of the curve that no data covers, which is why the parameter attracts governance rather than merely scrutiny.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'The b-leverage table runs 91666.6666666667, 112653.225353287, 142264.973081037, 185086.013635292, 248490.664978800 and 321875.914758613 stb at b of 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1 and 1.2. What shape does that describe?', '["Linear growth of about 20000 stb per 0.25 of b", "Growth that flattens above b of 0.75 as the curve approaches the harmonic limit at b of 1, beyond which EUR is unbounded", "A step change at b of 1, with little movement on either side of it", "Accelerating growth: the increments run 20986.558686620294, 29611.74772775, 42821.040554255014 and 63404.65134350798 stb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each 0.25 of b is worth more than the one before, so a small upward adjustment made late in an evaluation is worth more than the same adjustment made early. Assuming the sensitivity is linear understates the exposure at high b.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'A colleague reports an EUR ratio of 3.5113736155485 for a well and offers it as evidence that the hyperbolic model fits better. What is wrong with that reasoning?', '["The ratio is inverted, so it should be reported as 0.28479 rather than as a multiple", "The ratio compares two bookings from the same data and says nothing about fit quality", "The ratio only applies at a 10 stb/d limit and changes sign at higher limits", "The ratio is a property of the economic limit rather than of the decline exponent, so it cannot support a claim about either"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both curves are fitted to the same early rows and both can track them closely. The multiple is a statement about how much extra volume the higher exponent extrapolates, not about which curve describes the history better.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Why does SPEE''s recommended evaluation practice single out the decline exponent rather than the initial rate or the nominal decline?', '["Because it is the only parameter that can multiply a booking without contradicting any observed data", "Because it is the only parameter the engine estimates by search rather than by regression", "Because it is the only parameter that is dimensionless", "Because it is the only parameter that changes when the economic limit is changed, which makes it the natural governance target"]'::jsonb, 0, 'qi and Di are pinned by the history you fitted, and a wrong value shows up as residuals. A higher b can honour the same history while adding volume in a tail that no data reaches, so the check has to be external to the fit.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Why do practitioners impose a terminal decline on a hyperbolic forecast?', '["Because the engine''s grid search becomes unstable at b above 1.2", "Because the economic limit of 10 stb/d cannot be reached by a hyperbolic curve at any finite time, so the forecast never terminates", "Because reserves auditors require every forecast to end on the same effective decline", "Because a hyperbolic decline flattens without bound, so its late-life rates stop describing any physical drive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The convention replaces the hyperbolic tail with an exponential one below a chosen decline rate. It is a governance device rather than a fitting improvement, and the memo has to record that it was applied and at what rate.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'The Ekene-1 primary-window fit returns confidence-interval half-widths of 2.74326145200774e-14 on qi and 3.69199091999372e-19 on Di. What does that tell you?', '["That the well is unusually well characterised, so its fitted parameters can safely be carried to nearby wells with no history of their own", "That the interval calculation has failed and should be reported as unavailable", "That the data carries no scatter, which is a statement about the data rather than about the truth of the parameters", "That the fitted b of 0 is correct to within the same precision"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An interval measures dispersion of the residuals, and perfect data gives a near-zero interval. It says nothing about whether the window, the family or the extrapolation is right, which is why intervals are taught on scattered literature data instead.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'A fit on real, scattered data returns a wide confidence interval on the decline exponent. Which statement does that licence?', '["That the model family is probably wrong and another should be tried", "That the data does not pin the exponent down, so the booking should carry the sensitivity", "That the true exponent lies inside the interval with the stated probability, whatever window was used", "That the window is too short, since interval width falls with the number of rows regardless of scatter"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The interval is conditional on the window, the family and the error model you assumed. Within those assumptions it says how little the data constrains the parameter, which is exactly the statement a reserves memo needs.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Ekene-1''s primary-window fit reports an RMSE of 1.42601125915484e-14 stb/d. What does a residual of that size establish?', '["That the fitted parameters are the true ones for the whole life of the well", "That the engine''s numerical precision is the limiting factor rather than the data", "That the well''s decline will not change, since nothing in the residuals suggests an approaching regime shift", "That the curve passes through those rows to machine precision, and nothing about rows outside the window"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A perfect fit over a window is a statement about that window only. The same well''s full-history fit reaches R2 0.818388421218434 with a wholly different set of parameters, on data that includes the flood.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Which pair of ideas does the confidence-and-scatter module insist on separating?', '["How closely the curve tracks your rows, and how tightly the data pins the parameters", "The nominal decline and the effective decline", "The quality tier and the R2 it is computed from", "The window''s start boundary and its end boundary, which are set by different classes of event"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fit can track its rows perfectly while leaving a parameter almost unconstrained, and it can pin a parameter tightly while describing the wrong regime. Goodness of fit and parameter precision answer different questions.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'A partner''s evaluation of the same Ekene triangle reports a P90 of 531360.331525141 stb and a P10 of 420425.025054486 stb. What has happened?', '["The partner has used a lognormal distribution, which reverses the ordering of the two quantiles", "The partner is labelling quantiles by probability of non-exceedance, the reverse of the petroleum convention", "The partner has swapped the minimum and the maximum of the triangle before inverting the cumulative distribution function", "The partner has computed both quantiles from the upper branch of the inverse cumulative distribution function"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two numbers are correct and the labels are exchanged. Petroleum practice names quantiles by probability of exceedance, so the low case carries the high label, and a memo that does not state which convention it follows invites exactly this.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The Expert capstone grades the field triangle''s P90 and P10 but neither its P50 nor its mean. What does that pairing test?', '["That the mode was entered correctly, since both quantiles depend on it", "That you can reproduce the closed-form quantiles the panel reports for the mode", "That you can produce both branches of the inverse and label them by the petroleum convention", "That the field total was carried forward from the Professional capstone without being recomputed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A quantile below the cumulative probability at the mode comes from one branch and one above it from the other, so the pair exercises both. Getting one right and the other inverted is the characteristic failure the pairing is designed to catch.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The Ekene triangle runs from 380000 to 580000 stb with a mode of 461709.132532792 stb. Which arithmetic on those three inputs tells you the distribution is skewed right?', '["The mode sits 81709.132532792 stb above the minimum and 118290.867467208 stb below the maximum", "The mean of 473903.044177597 stb lies above the mode, which is the definition of right skew for a triangle", "The range of 200000 stb is more than 40 percent of the mode, which is the threshold for a skewed triangle", "The P90 of 420425.025054486 stb lies closer to the minimum than the P10 of 531360.331525141 stb lies to the maximum"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The longer arm is the upper one, so mass is pulled toward the maximum. That single comparison, made before any quantile is computed, predicts both that the mean will exceed the mode and that the cumulative probability at the mode will be below one half.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'The Ekene-6 memo says any credible range on that booking must come from a range on water cut rather than from the decline fit. Why can the triangular machinery not supply it?', '["Because a triangular distribution requires a symmetric mode, which a water-cut trajectory does not have", "Because water cut is bounded between 0 and 1, so no continuous distribution over it can be integrated to a volume", "Because the panel''s triangle is hard-wired to the field EUR inputs and cannot be repointed at a well", "Because the fit''s residual scatter is negligible and the exposure lies in a curve the decline analysis never modelled"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A distribution can only carry uncertainty over a quantity the model contains. Ekene-6''s oil booking is dominated by an extrapolated water-cut trajectory that nobody forecast, so the honest range has to come from displacement and sweep work rather than from the decline statistics.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The field triangle''s P90 is 420425.025054486 stb and its P10 is 531360.331525141 stb. What is the reported range, and what does it represent?', '["110935.30647065502 stb, the spread of the four wells'' individual EURs", "110935.30647065502 stb, about 23.5 percent of the P50, describing the field outcome rather than any one well", "150935 stb, the difference between the maximum and the minimum of the triangle", "110935.30647065502 stb, which is the uncertainty contributed by the decline exponent alone across the four wells"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The range is a statement about the field total under the stated distribution. It is not a sum of well-level ranges, and it carries no information about which input drives it until the inputs are moved one at a time.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'What is the argument for reporting reserves as a distribution rather than as a single number?', '["A single number is only valid at a stated economic limit, while a distribution is limit-independent", "Regulators require three cases regardless of how they were produced", "A single value carries no information about how wrong it might be, or in which direction", "A distribution removes the need to justify the decline exponent, since its effect is absorbed into the range"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The point is not to avoid committing to a number. It is to make the commitment carry its own error bars, so that a later revision is an update rather than an admission.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'In the Ekene response model, what happens to a producer''s primary Arps decline at the flood start?', '["It is replaced: the rate on 2023-01-01 is frozen as a base and the response is built from that value", "It continues underneath the flood response, which is added on top as an increment", "It is refitted with a higher b, which is how the model represents pressure support", "It continues at the same nominal decline but with the initial rate reset to the flood-start rate of the well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The lift multiplies the frozen base rather than the current rate, which is why the peaks are exactly the base times 1.35 and 1.15 for Ekene-6 and Ekene-5. If the decline continued underneath, those peaks would not land on round multiples.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'Which Ekene producer has the smallest wcMax and the latest water breakthrough?', '["Ekene-5, which has a wcMax of 0 and no breakthrough date at all", "Ekene-3, with a wcMax of 0.25 and a breakthrough on 2024-09-01", "Ekene-6, with a wcMax of 0.45 and a breakthrough on 2024-03-01", "Ekene-1, with a wcMax of 0.08 and a breakthrough on 2025-06-01"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-5 has no breakthrough at all, so it has no breakthrough date to be latest. Among the wells that do water out, the one furthest from an injector responds last and drowns least.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'Ekene-5''s first clean post-ramp start is 2024-04-01. What is special about that particular row?', '["It is the first row after the flood on which the rate falls rather than rises", "It is the ramp-end row itself, being the lag of 9 months plus the ramp of 6 months after the flood start", "It is the first row at which R2 crosses the Excellent threshold of 0.95", "It is the first row on which the fitted qi falls below the peak rate of the response"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fifteen months after 2023-01-01 the ramp is complete and the well is a clean exponential from that row onward. Every later start recovers the same constant with a lower initial rate, which is why the graded quantity is the decline and not the rate.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Ekene-3''s clean post-ramp window, 2024-01-01 to 2024-08-01, returns qi 45.231993083244205 stb/d and Di 0.0003499999999999994 per day at R2 1. Ekene-5''s returns 47.59933774834437 stb/d and 0.0003500000000000004 per day. What does the pair establish?', '["That Ekene-3 and Ekene-5 are draining a common compartment separate from the other two producers", "That the two wells are close enough in decline character to be pooled into a type curve", "That the post-ramp decline is a field parameter, recovered independently from different windows", "That the difference in the fifteenth digit is a real difference in decline, too small to book"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Four windows of different lengths, from 5 rows to 21, and different start dates return the same constant. The trailing digits differ because the fits accumulate floating-point error differently, not because the wells do.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'On the 2024-05-01 window, Ekene-3''s oil decline is 2.3226835595128104 times the reservoir''s and Ekene-6''s is 3.793112425481473 times. What orders the two?', '["How much of the window carries water and how fast the cut climbs, wcMax being 0.25 and 0.45", "The distance from the injectors, which is 1216.5525060596437 m and 715.8910531638176 m", "The lift factors of 1.25 and 1.35, which set the height the post-ramp curve starts from", "The planted decline exponents of 0.5 and 0.35, which survive into the flood regime"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-3 has water on 15 of its 20 rows and Ekene-6 on all 20, and Ekene-6''s cut climbs almost twice as far. The distortion tracks the water, and everything else about the two wells is common to both on that window.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Booking Ekene-6''s post-flood life from its oil fit gives 36257.11302016905 stb, while its gross fit gives 124290.17930382918 stb, a factor of 3.428021950746306. What is the honest reading of that gap?', '["The oil figure is too low and should be replaced by the gross figure", "The two are alternative bookings and either may be reported provided the choice is stated", "The gap measures the sweep efficiency of the flood at this well", "Neither is a booking: one is a liquid volume, and the other embeds an unstated water-cut forecast"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gross figure needs a water-cut forecast before it becomes oil. The oil figure already contains one, arriving invisibly through the fitted decline constant, and the size of the gap is how much that hidden assumption is worth.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Ekene-1 carries water on only 6 of the 20 rows in the capstone window and its wcMax is 0.08, yet its oil fit returns 0.00043602810153168224 per day. What does that show?', '["That the fit has failed, since a wcMax that small cannot move a decline constant", "That the distortion begins with the first water, and reaches 24.57945758048064 percent here", "That six rows are too few for the engine to fit reliably", "That the well is in a separate compartment where the flood decline is genuinely steeper than 0.00035 per day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fit is certified Excellent at R2 0.9612773701829425 while the decline is a quarter too steep. A well does not have to feel wet for a rising cut to bend its oil stream away from the reservoir''s behaviour.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'Where does tool validation sit in the Expert workflow?', '["After the fit, as a cross-check on the parameters obtained", "Alongside the roll-up, since portfolio totals are where errors compound", "Before anything else, because nothing downstream is worth anything if the arithmetic is wrong", "At submission, as a supporting appendix to the reserves memo"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The step is numbered ahead of the regime log deliberately. A validation performed after a result exists is at risk of being run until it agrees, which is the same failure mode as building the regime log after the fit.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'A reviewer accepts a window because the fit reports R2 0.9887 and a quality tier of Excellent. What is the Expert objection?', '["The statistic averages over the window, so it cannot detect the contamination it is averaging", "The threshold should be set at 0.99 for flooded wells", "R2 is undefined for exponential fits and RMSE should be quoted instead", "The tier bands were calibrated on noise-free data and are therefore too lenient for any real dataset"]'::jsonb, 0, 'That exact fit is Ekene-5 with one row of ramp still in the window, returning a decline 3.66 percent low. The test that works is stability: move the start and see whether the constant moves.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Under the Expert workflow, a well whose water cut is climbing should be handled how?', '["Fit the oil stream with a hyperbolic model, so the curvature is captured by the exponent", "Fit the oil stream but shorten the window until the water-bearing rows are excluded", "Fit the oil stream and record the resulting decline as the well''s reservoir decline", "Fit the gross stream, forecast water cut separately, and multiply the two"]'::jsonb, 3, 'That construction puts the water assumption in the model as a named line that a reviewer can disagree with. Absorbing it into a decline constant, or into an exponent, hides a claim about future water inside a reserves number.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Why do reserves auditors read the memo clause on what would change the answer first?', '["Because it is the only clause that is not reproducible from the data", "Because it shows whether the author knows what the number depends on", "Because it establishes whether the booking is proved, probable or possible", "Because it lists the exclusions, which is where undisclosed facility constraints usually appear"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A number with a named sensitivity list can be revised in an orderly way when new data arrives. A number without one has to be defended as it stands, because nobody recorded what would move it.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Ekene-5''s post-flood booking is 106006.16987382741 stb over 4427.8107294189585 days at a 10 stb/d limit. Which single feature of this well makes that booking the clean case in the field?', '["Its harmonic primary decline, which flattens the tail and makes the post-ramp fit less sensitive to the window", "Its lag of 9 months, which leaves the longest clean window in the field", "Its wcMax of 0, which makes the oil stream and the gross stream the same series", "Its lift of 1.15, the smallest in the field, which keeps the post-ramp curve closest to the primary decline"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With no water there is no stream conversion to argue about, so the fitted decline is the reservoir''s and the booking needs no water-cut forecast. That is also why it serves as the reference against which the other three wells are read.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The Expert capstone grades an EUR ratio near 3.51 to a tolerance of 0.02, which is about 0.57 percent of the value. What does a tolerance that tight require of you?', '["That both EURs come from the same convention and the same fixed qi, Di and limit", "That the ratio be computed from the engine''s daily forecast sums rather than the closed forms", "That the ratio be reported to at least six significant figures", "That the exponential booking be taken from the Associate capstone submission rather than recomputed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A ratio is only as reproducible as the pair it comes from. Mixing a closed-form numerator with a daily-sum denominator, or moving the limit between them, shifts the result by more than the band allows.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'A colleague submits 0.00035 per day for the capstone field asking for Ekene-6''s oil decline on the 2024-05-01 window, arguing it is the physically correct number. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing: 0.00035 is within the tolerance of 0.00002 for that field", "The value is right but the units are wrong, since the field is graded per year", "The window is wrong; 0.00035 would be correct on a window ending before breakthrough", "The field asks what a fit of that well''s oil stream returns, which is a different quantity from the reservoir''s decline"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The graded value is 3.793112425481473 times larger, so the submission misses by far more than the band. Knowing that the reservoir declines at 0.00035 per day is the reason the memo is honest, not a reason to change the reported fit.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Which statement about the memo''s window clause is correct?', '["Only the start boundary needs a physical justification, since the end is set by data availability", "It has to record the stability experiment and its result, not the intention behind the window", "It should quote the R2 of every candidate window examined", "It can be omitted when the fit reports R2 of exactly 1, since a perfect fit cannot span two regimes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A clause that records a conclusion tells a reviewer what you wanted and nothing about whether you got it. An end boundary set by a breakthrough or a workover is as much a model statement as the start.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'The Ekene fixture''s material-balance history uses the exact closed-form cumulatives of the four planted declines. Why does that choice matter to a decline analyst?', '["Because material balance requires cumulatives to be monotonic, which snapshot sums are not", "Because the engine''s forecast routine cannot produce cumulatives at arbitrary survey dates", "Because the cumulative convention you choose propagates directly into the estimate of oil in place", "Because the pressure inversion in the fixture is only valid for cumulatives computed by integration"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Underground withdrawal is computed from the cumulative, and oil in place is the slope of withdrawal against expansion. A cumulative built by a different route moves the answer to a question decline analysis cannot otherwise touch.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'The Havlena-Odeh regression on the Ekene surveys returns 12139208.107496837 stb against a volumetric STOIIP of 12139208.107496763 stb, a relative difference of 6.137616664074325e-15. What should a student take from that?', '["That the fixture is a closed loop by construction, so the disagreement a real field shows is the informative case", "That material balance is more accurate than volumetric mapping when six or more surveys are available", "That the volumetric estimate can be dispensed with once a material balance is available", "That an R2 of 1 on the Havlena-Odeh line proves the drive model is correct"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The dynamic history was built as the exact consequence of the volumetric answer so that a closed loop can be seen before an open one. On a real field the gap is the study''s most useful signal, and the cumulative is one of its three suspects.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'By the flood start the Ekene field had produced 261475.039999678 stb and pressure had fallen from 3200 psia to 2096.00826266700 psia. What is the recovery factor implied?', '["21.53971146093131 percent", "About 5 percent, being the pressure drop as a fraction of the initial pressure", "It cannot be computed without the formation volume factor at the flood start", "2.153971146093131 percent of the 12139208.107496763 stb in place"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two percent of the oil recovered for a third of the initial pressure spent, with the tank only 96.0082626669955 psi above its bubble point, is the reason the waterflood exists at all.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'A booking is submitted with a fitted decline, an EUR, a limit and a stated window, but no note of which forecast convention produced the volume. What is the practical consequence?', '["None, provided the closed form and the engine forecast agree to better than one percent", "The next engineer can land near your number but cannot land on it", "The booking cannot be audited at all until the convention is supplied", "The volume must be recomputed from the daily sum, which is the convention auditors assume by default"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both conventions are legitimate and they differ. The clause protects reproducibility, which is the property that distinguishes a submitted number from an estimate somebody remembers making.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Which of these belongs in a memo''s exclusion list rather than in its uncertainty clause?', '["The width of the confidence interval on the fitted decline exponent", "The probability that the economic limit is reached later than forecast", "That no produced-water handling constraint is modelled", "The spread between the P90 and P10 of the field distribution"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exclusion list names what the forecast does not contain at all, while the uncertainty clause quantifies the spread of what it does contain. A constraint that is absent from the model is not a wide case within it.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Which task does decline curve analysis structurally not perform?', '["Estimating a recoverable volume at a stated economic limit", "Estimating the time at which a well reaches a stated rate", "Estimating the effective annual decline of a producing stream", "Estimating how much oil is in the reservoir"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every output here is an extrapolation of a rate, conditional on the drive mechanism continuing as observed. A decline-based volume can be perfectly fitted, fully documented and still exceed the oil in place, which is why the volume-based check is a separate discipline.'),
  ('dca', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'What is the single habit the Expert tier is trying to leave you with?', '["Preferring closed forms to engine forecasts wherever both exist", "Asking, of any output, what regime it describes, what stream it was measured on, and what would make it wrong", "Fitting every well on at least twenty rows before booking it", "Reporting every parameter at full double precision so that nothing is lost to rounding"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The arithmetic in this course is elementary and the judgment is not. That question transfers unchanged to material balance, to displacement work and to simulation, where the models are larger and the assumptions are harder to see.');
